# {{ chart_name | default("A2A Erlang Application") }}

{{ description | default("A2A Erlang Application Helm Chart") }}

## TL;DR

```bash
helm repo add a2a {{ repository | default("https://a2a.github.io/helm-charts") }}
helm install {{ chart_name | default("a2a-erl") }} a2a/{{ chart_name | default("a2a-erl") }}
```

## Introduction

This chart bootstraps a {{ chart_name | default("a2a-erl") }} deployment on a [Kubernetes](https://kubernetes.io) cluster using the [Helm](https://helm.sh) package manager.

## Prerequisites

- Kubernetes {{ kube_version | default(">= 1.19.0") }}
- Helm 3.x

## Installing the Chart

To install the chart with the release name `my-release`:

```bash
helm install my-release a2a/{{ chart_name | default("a2a-erl") }}
```

The command deploys {{ chart_name | default("a2a-erl") }} on the Kubernetes cluster in the default configuration. The [configuration](#configuration) section lists the parameters that can be configured during installation.

> **Tip**: List all releases using `helm list`

## Uninstalling the Chart

To uninstall/delete the `my-release` deployment:

```bash
helm delete my-release
```

The command removes all the Kubernetes components associated with the chart and deletes the release.

## Configuration

The following table lists the configurable parameters of the {{ chart_name | default("a2a-erl") }} chart and their default values.

| Parameter | Description | Default |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `replicaCount` | Number of replicas | `2` |
| `image.repository` | Image repository | `ghcr.io/a2a/a2a-erl` |
| `image.tag` | Image tag | `v0.2.0` |
| `service.type` | Service type | `ClusterIP` |
| `service.port` | Service port | `8080` |
| `resources.requests.cpu` | CPU requests | `100m` |
| `resources.requests.memory` | Memory requests | `128Mi` |
| `resources.limits.cpu` | CPU limits | `500m` |
| `resources.limits.memory` | Memory limits | `256Mi` |

Specify each parameter using the `--set key=value[,key=value]` argument to `helm install`. For example,

```bash
helm install my-release a2a/{{ chart_name | default("a2a-erl") }} --set replicaCount=3
```

Alternatively, a YAML file that specifies the values for the parameters can be provided while installing the chart. For example,

```bash
helm install my-release a2a/{{ chart_name | default("a2a-erl") }} -f values.yaml
```

> **Tip**: You can refer to the [values.yaml](./values.yaml) file to see the default values.

## Configuration and Chart Parameters

### HotCI Configuration

The chart supports HotCI (Hot Code Loading CI) configuration:

```yaml
hotci:
  enabled: true
  checkInterval: "30s"
  upgradeValidation: true
  rollbackTimeout: 30000
  monitoringInterval: "10s"
```

### Application Configuration

Application-specific configuration:

```yaml
application:
  httpPort: 8080
  enableSSE: true
  enableMetrics: true
  logLevel: info
  environment:
    NODE_ENV: production
```

### Monitoring Configuration

Prometheus monitoring configuration:

```yaml
monitoring:
  enabled: true
  port: 8080
  path: "/metrics"
  serviceMonitor:
    enabled: true
    interval: "30s"
    scrapeTimeout: "10s"
```

### Network Policy

Network policy configuration:

```yaml
networkPolicy:
  enabled: true
  ingress:
    - from:
        - podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: a2a-erl
        namespaceSelector:
          matchLabels:
            name: a2a-system
```

### Backup Configuration

Backup configuration:

```yaml
backup:
  enabled: true
  schedule: "0 2 * * *"
  retention: "7d"
  storageClass: standard
```

### Service Mesh Integration

Istio service mesh integration:

```yaml
serviceMesh:
  enabled: true
  injectAnnotation: "true"
  gateway:
    enabled: true
    host: "a2a.example.com"
    port: 80
```

## Resources

| CPU | Memory | Description |
| --- | --- | --- |
| {{ resources.requests.cpu | default("100m") }} | {{ resources.requests.memory | default("128Mi") }} | Minimum resources for pod |
| {{ resources.limits.cpu | default("500m") }} | {{ resources.limits.memory | default("256Mi") }} | Maximum resources for pod |

## Persistence

The chart does not create any persistent volume claims by default. If you need persistent storage for your application, you can configure it in the `values.yaml` file:

```yaml
volumes:
  - name: data
    persistentVolumeClaim:
      claimName: a2a-data

volumeMounts:
  - name: data
    mountPath: /app/data
```

## Troubleshooting

### Events

To list events for the last hour:

```bash
kubectl get events --field-selector involvedObject.name=my-release-{{ chart_name | default("a2a-erl") }} --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp
```

### Logs

To view application logs:

```bash
kubectl logs -f deployment/my-release-{{ chart_name | default("a2a-erl") }}
```

### Pod Information

To describe pod information:

```bash
kubectl describe pod my-release-{{ chart_name | default("a2a-erl") }}-xxxxx
```

### Debug Shell

To get a shell in a running pod:

```bash
kubectl exec -it my-release-{{ chart_name | default("a2a-erl") }}-xxxxx -- /bin/sh
```

## Support

- For issues with the chart, please open an issue [here](https://github.com/a2a/helm-charts/issues)
- For general questions about the A2A Erlang application, see the [documentation](https://docs.a2a.com)

